00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.890
How You Can Use Them
With GKE + Istio,"

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.870
then stay tuned, because
here's the rewind.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.385 --> 00:00:22.260
When your critical cloud
applications are up

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.820
and running, you
cannot afford downtime.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:26.970
And so you probably have
many systems in place

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.310
to prevent and monitor
your apps, to find out

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.840
when they aren't happy.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.729
Thankfully, things can
get a little easier

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:37.360
for you using a service mesh and
standard reliability practices.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:38.850
OK, so what's a service mesh?

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.410
A service mesh is
a way to automate

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:44.220
key functions of your network
for interrelated applications.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.140
And to accomplish
that, it's critical

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.200
that this tool be transparent
and language-agnostic,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.350
giving you increased flexibility
instead of more integration

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:53.900
headaches.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.060
Think of a service mesh
as an open platform

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.310
to manage how services
interact, and give

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:01.130
you insight into how they
depend on each other.

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:03.110
It does all of this
by adding a sidecar

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:07.250
proxy, either a container or
a little buddy for your VM

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.770
that will handle inbound
and outbound traffic,

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.577
apply policies, and
collect metrics.

00:01:12.577 --> 00:01:15.160
But don't worry, you don't need
to build all this on your own.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.630
Istio is an open
source service mesh

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:19.430
that you can use
for great monitoring

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:22.460
right from the start, providing
key metrics for observing

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.400
the health of your services,
like traffic, errors,

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.170
and latency.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.790
Each key metric
is useful to help

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:31.520
you track if services
outages are happening,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.730
or where a disruption might be
occurring in your service mesh.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:38.390
With Istio, you get automated
collection and dashboarding

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:42.060
of traffic errors and
latency right out of the box.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.200
You can export these logs
into BigQuery, Stackdriver,

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.830
or any third party tool that you
like, and do precise analysis,

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.510
alerting, and reporting.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.610
That way, you and your
SREs can understand

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.730
exactly what's happening in your
system when there is an issue.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.620
Using Istio, you
can also roll out

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.750
new versions of a
service carefully,

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.450
deploying a canary test for
analysis and measurement

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:04.550
before you roll
out to all traffic.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.130
This improves on basic
Kubernetes functionality,

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.530
which would split traffic
across all pods evenly.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.790
But with Istio's tools, you
can say, test this new pod

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:16.130
with 1% of traffic,
without having

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:18.350
to deploy exactly 100 pods.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.080
To hear more about
reliability best practices,

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.510
Dickerson's Hierarchy
of Reliability Needs,

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.050
as well as SLOs, SLAs,
and SLIs, check out

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:28.970
the full talk at the
Cloud YouTube channel,

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:31.370
and don't forget to subscribe
for more great NEXT Rewind

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:32.040
content.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.790
Thanks for watching.

